.TH CBMAP_INIT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init_cc \- create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init_cc(hm_name, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map whose keys custom_comparison_proc orders instead
of the default order for the declared key type, and assigns the map to
hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with cbmap_declare(3) or
cbmap_declare_scoped(3). The key type and the value type do not come to
this macro as arguments: the macro reads them from the companion type
variables that the declare macro created.

The macro is cbmap_init(3) plus a comparison procedure, and it uses the
default allocator. cbmap_init_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead, and
cbmap_init_full(3) takes both.

A struct key type needs this variant. Without a procedure, the map orders a
key type that it does not recognize by a raw comparison of the
representation, which reads padding bytes and orders a pointer member by
its address.

The one-step equivalents are cbmap_construct_cc(3) and
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 The map handle that the caller declared before,
                        into which the macro creates the map
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for the declared key type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key, a positive value when the
first key orders after the second key, and zero when the map must treat
the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update instead of an
insert, so the procedure defines both key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over the key type, and that
order must not change while the map holds elements: the same pair of keys
must always compare in the same way, a comparison in the opposite direction
must give the opposite sign, and the order must be transitive. An
inconsistent procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree, after
which the map cannot find a key that it holds.

Because the procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, it can only
read a width that the declared key type fixes. The map therefore validates
the width of an incoming key against that type and aligns its stored keys
for that type; the procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing
else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro uses the default allocator
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
The order of the walk follows this procedure: the walk visits the elements
in ascending key order, by the order that the map got, as
cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_declare(releases, version, char *);
cbmap_init_cc(releases, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
